# Prefer real types over ad-hoc shapes + casts

When a function operates on a recursive or already-modeled structure, its
parameter types must mirror the actual structure it expects — not a
loosely-typed stand-in that requires an internal cast to be usable.

If you find yourself writing `as typeof X` (or any cast) to make a narrowed
inline type behave like the real one, that's a signal the parameter type is
wrong. Fix the type, don't cast around it. `satisfies` checks; if it fails,
stop and ask — see `no-unsafe-casts.mdc`.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — inline shape with `unknown[][]` children can't actually recurse;
// requires a cast to "restore" the real type when descending.
const walk = (
  columns: { header: { deleted: boolean }; meta: { index: number }; children: unknown[][] }[][],
) => {
  for (const node of columns.flat()) {
    if (!node.header.deleted) walk(node.children as typeof columns);
  }
};

// ✅ GOOD — use the library's real recursive type; `node.children` is already
// that same type, so no cast is needed to recurse.
const walk = (
  columns: RecursiveFormItem<TypeNames, Params, MetaDom<SIndexed>>[][],
) => {
  for (const node of columns.flat()) {
    if (!node.header.deleted) walk(node.children);
  }
};
```

Guidelines:

- Search for an existing type (e.g. `RecursiveFormItem`, `SectionWithItems`)
  before inventing a local inline object type for a function parameter.
- A parameter typed to accept `X[]` (or `unknown[]`) should genuinely be able
  to handle any value of type `X` — if the function body assumes a narrower
  shape, that narrower shape belongs in the parameter type.
- Prefer generic parameters matching the module's existing generics
  (`TypeNames`, `Params`, `Meta`, `SectionConfig`, …) over hardcoding concrete
  field lists.

## Don't "genericize" a hardcoded assumption into a floating one

If a function hardcodes concrete field names that the domain type doesn't
actually guarantee (e.g. assuming a `Section` has `title`/`description` when
the real type only requires `id`/`deleted`), making it generic over those
field names is **not** a fix if the generic has no real link back to the
domain type. That just moves the same untyped assumption one level down —
often introducing a genuine cast (`Object.keys(x) as (keyof T)[]`) along the
way, which this rule already flags as a smell.

Ask: once genericized, does this function still need to know anything about
the real domain type (`SectionConfig`, `SectionDom`, …)? If **no** — it only
operates on whatever shape you hand it — it isn't domain-specific logic and
doesn't belong as typed "library" API for that domain. Either:

- tie the generic to the real shared type (e.g. `Fields extends
  Pick<SectionConfig, ...>`, constrained by an actual type used elsewhere in
  the module), so mismatches are real compile errors, or
- if it truly needs no domain knowledge, don't dress it up as reusable
  library code — inline the few lines where the concrete shape is already
  known (usually the demo/host), and delete the "generic" version.

Example: `validateSectionForm({ title, description })` hardcoded to school's
field names → "genericized" to `Fields extends Record<string, string>` with
`Fields` inferred only from the `errorMessages` argument. Nothing tied the
validated keys back to any real `SectionConfig`, so a host could pass field
names that don't exist on its actual header with no type error — and a field
named `cols` silently collided with the fixed `cols` slot, producing `never`.
The fix was to delete it and let the one caller that actually knows its
concrete `Section` shape (the demo) write the four-line check inline.

**Closed for `section-edit`:** do not reintroduce a library `validateSectionForm`.
Host dialogs own required-field checks against their concrete form type.

## Generic factories can escape indexed-access assignability

TypeScript is stricter when checking a value against a **concrete**
instantiation of a polymorphic type than when the same body is checked under
an unbound type parameter.

```typescript
type M = { a: number; b: string };
type Mapper<
  Keys extends PropertyKey,
  Src extends Record<Keys, unknown>,
  Dst extends Record<Keys, unknown>,
> = <K extends Keys>(x: Src[K]) => Dst[K];

type Dst<M> = { [K in keyof M]: Promise<M[K]> };

// ❌ Direct assign — Promise<M[K]> is not assignable to Dst<M>[K]
//    (indexed access on a concrete mapped type does not reduce).
const mapper: Mapper<keyof M, M, Dst<M>> = async (x) => x;

// ✅ Factory — body is checked while M is still a type parameter; Dst<M>[K]
//    unifies with Promise<M[K]>. Instantiating afterward is fine.
const makeMapper = <M,>(): Mapper<keyof M, M, Dst<M>> => async (x) => x;
const mapperOk = makeMapper<M>();
```

Same pitfall shows up with per-key maps whose value types **differ across
keys** (e.g. `isError: (param: keyof Params[K]) => boolean` when only some
`Params[K]` include `"required"`): assigning a generic hook body to a
concrete `UseFormItemEditor<…, ItemStateMap>` fails; returning that body from
`makeUse…<TypeNames, Params>()` succeeds without a cast.

Prefer the factory form over `as` / `as unknown as` when you hit this
pattern. Do **not** confuse this with inventing floating generics that drop
domain constraints — see “Don't genericize a hardcoded assumption” above.
The factory's type parameters must still be the real maps (`TypeNames`,
`Params`, `ItemStateMap`, …).

Related: never switch on concrete item type names outside viewers/editors —
see `no-per-type-extension.mdc` (`withFormItemName` / `cloneFlatItems`).
